      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think my comments below would avoid the issue of someone calling tc_start_event_loop after the suspend, but I think that you should use a lock here to make sure that after calling pause nobody can start while you\u0027re waiting for things to stabilize.\n\nSo basically:\n\n- lock\n- tc_pause_event_loop\n- wait for tc_event_loop_is_paused (which should use a condvar instead of just sleeping/yielding)\n- k_thread_suspend\n- unlock\n\n(same with usbc_pd_task_resume)",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 60,
        "startChar": 2,
        "endLine": 60,
        "endChar": 14
      },
      "revId": "4e34f1b359881927d69e60e3073324ea3b64d950",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "016a3555_7110a34c",
        "filename": "common/usbc/usbc_task.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 60,
      "author": {
        "id": 1425418
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-10-20T15:46:34Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Not sure I agree with the usage of a condvar for suspending because one of the things that does occur is the pd task will reach a paused state before we ask it to unpause, if it has reached that state before the test thread waits on a condvar, the condvar will never get signaled.\n\nI see two straight forward ways to handle that scenario:\n\n1) I could add some state to track this and handle appropriately, but I\u0027ve been trying to avoid state as much as possible here.\n\n2) I could make a call to tc_start_event_loop and then subsequently ask it to pause and signal on our own terms. I think this would be fine, but it seems unnecessary to me when pausing a paused task should be a noop (besides keeping it from starting again).",
      "parentUuid": "4e3f961b_c8279323",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 60,
        "startChar": 2,
        "endLine": 60,
        "endChar": 14
      },
